rizontal line y = 2 includes B,E (and maybe (n-2,2) and (n-1,2)). That's 2 points.\n\n- Horizontal line y = 3 includes C only.\n\nThus the only non-sunny lines covering 3 points are: vertical x=n-2 (covers A,B,C), horizontal y=1 (covers A,D,F), diagonal x+y=n+1 (covers C,E,F). Also maybe vertical x=n-1 covers D,E (2 points). Vertical x=n covers F only.\n\nThus we have three possible choices for the non-sunny line covering 3 points. Let's examine each case.\n\nCase A: Non-sunny line = vertical x=n-2 covering A,B,C. Then remaining points are D,E,F. Need to cover D,E,F with two sunny lines, each covering at most 2 points. Since we have three points left, we need at least two lines, but each line can cover at most 2 points, so we need to cover them with one line covering 2 points and another covering 1 point. However, we need to check if any pair among D,E,F can be covered by a sunny line. D = (n-1,1), E = (n-1,2), F = (n,1). Pairs: D-E vertical (non-sunny), D-F horizontal (non-sunny), E-F slope = (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1, non-sunny. So no pair can be covered by a sunny line. So we would need three sunny lines to cover D,E,F individually, but we only have two. So case A fails.\n\nCase B: Non-sunny line = horizontal y=1 covering A,D,F. Remaining points: B,C,E. B = (n-2,2), C = (n-2,3), E = (n-1,2). Check pairs: B-C vertical (non-sunny), B-E slope = (2-2)/(n-1-(n-2)) = 0, horizontal (non-sunny). C-E slope = (2-3)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -1, diagonal (non-sunny). So again no pair can be covered by a sunny line. So case B fails.\n\nCase C: Non-sunny line = diagonal x+y=n+1 covering C,E,F. Remaining points: A,B,D. A = (n-2,1), B = (n-2,2), D = (n-1,1). Pairs: A-B vertical (non-sunny), A-D horizontal (non-sunny), B-D slope = (1-2)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -1, diagonal (non-sunny). So no pair can be covered by a sunny line. So case C fails.\n\nThus any non-sunny line covering 3 points leaves three points that cannot be paired by a sunny line. So we cannot cover the rightmost three columns with exactly